  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 102 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSnow Showers Ending This Afternoon: Snow showers and flurries will\nend from northwest to southeast during the afternoon hours as a\npotent shortwave trough pushes east of the region. The shortwave,\nlow-level lapse rates from 7-8.5C/km and SBCAPEs up to 100 J/kg\nwill aid in light to moderate snow showers. A brief heavy snow\nshowers is also possible, as the snow squall parameter remains \nelevated, between 1-3. Main impacts will be rapidly changing \nvisibilities to under 1 mile, locally to around 1/2 mile at \ntimes. Road temps have remained above freezing, so we are not \nexpected any snow covered roads. In addition, there is not arctic \nfront associated with this system, so temps are only gradually \nfalling at the back end of the snow shower activity, so no flash \nfreeze conditions are expected. Have handled this threat with SPS \nand will continue to unless any one of the snow showers gets \nbetter organized. \n\nRest of the Week: Dry conditions are expected Tuesday into\nWednesday as high pressure slides across the Great Lakes. Pattern\nbecomes very amplified Wednesday night through Thanksgiving \nnight, as a closed low moves from the central Plains mid-week to\nthe Ohio Valley late in the week. Still uncertainty on how this\nwill interact/phase with a system on the East Coast. NBM still\nshowing 20-45% chance of rain/snow, highest across eastern WI,\nThanksgiving afternoon/evening. GEFS/AIGFS/AIFS/AIFS-ENS and \nAIGEFS all favor eastern WI just getting clipped on the western \nfringes of the system, but a few members still want to bring some \nsnow into the area. So something to watch, but will stick with the\nNBM that brings just a light rain/snow mix for now. A period of \ndry conditions returns as the large system departs to our east \nwith a weaker system possibly bringing some precip next weekend.\n\nTemperatures: The taste of typical late November conditions will\nstick around into Tuesday, with highs mainly in the 30s, which is\nslightly below normal. Temps slowly moderate mid-late week, with \nslightly above normal readings returning by late this week.",
